\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{A negative axis, site and link}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Keep the torus, the site reflection and the link reflection
of \ref{fdip-007M}, and the Hamiltonian of \ref{fdip-007O}.
Allow a weight to be negative, provided
#{w_{\theta e}=w_e}. If the precision is positive definite,
the site reflection is reflection positive. The link
reflection need not be. On the time circle
#{X=\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}}, with every weight #{w=-1} and
with #{m^2=5}, the link pairing of the field at time #{1} equals
#{-1/5}. Every edge weight is negative, so the derived form is
not positive definite, and the signature count of
\ref{fdip-00Q1} is not claimed.}

\proof{
\p{For the site reflection, times in #{\{1,\ldots,M-1\}} and
times in #{\{M+1,\ldots,2M-1\}} are separated by the fixed
times #{0} and #{M}. A nearest-neighbor edge changes one
coordinate by #{1}, so none joins those two open sets. Every
term of the Hamiltonian is supported in one closed half or on
the fixed set. Let #{H_+} be the sum of the terms supported in
the closed positive half but not wholly on the fixed set, and
let #{H_0} be the sum supported on the fixed set. Set
#{A=-H_+-\tfrac12 H_0}. Reflection exchanges #{H_+} with its
partner and fixes #{H_0}, using #{w_{\theta e}=w_e} and no
square root, so #{-H=A+\Theta A}. On the cube where every
coordinate lies in #{[-R,R]}, the summands are bounded, and
\ref{fdip-007N} gives reflection positivity of the cutoff
law. Positive definiteness bounds #{H} from below by a
positive multiple of #{\|\phi\|^2}. Polynomial observables
remain integrable, and their cutoff pairings tend to the
Gaussian pairing.}

\p{On #{X=\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}} each vertex meets two edges.
The diagonal entry of the precision is #{2w+m^2=3}, and each
neighbor entry is #{-w=1}. The matrix is the circulant with
first row #{(3,1,0,1)}. Its eigenvalues are
##{3+2\cos(\pi k/2)}
for #{k=0,1,2,3}, namely #{5,3,1,3}, hence positive. The
covariance at lag #{1} is
##{\frac14\Bigl(\frac15+0-\frac11+0\Bigr)=-\frac15.}
Time #{1} lies in the positive half of the link reflection
#{\theta(t)=1-t}, and #{\theta(1)=0}. The pairing of the
coordinate observable at time #{1} is this covariance.}
}
